# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Java Idiomatic Guide

## Concurrency

- Use virtual threads (Project Loom, 21+) for I/O-bound work: `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` — cheap, scale to millions, write blocking-style code
- Keep platform-thread pools for CPU-bound work; don't pool virtual threads
- Avoid `synchronized` blocks that span blocking calls on virtual threads (pinning) — prefer `ReentrantLock`
- Use `java.util.concurrent` primitives (`ConcurrentHashMap`, `CompletableFuture`, `Atomic*`) over hand-rolled locking
- Prefer immutable data shared across threads; confine mutable state to a single owner
- Use structured concurrency (`StructuredTaskScope`, preview) to scope and cancel related subtasks together

## Error Handling

- Use checked exceptions for recoverable conditions callers must handle; unchecked for programming errors
- Wrap-and-rethrow with a cause: `throw new ServiceException("context", e)` — never swallow the original
- Catch the narrowest exception type; never catch bare `Exception`/`Throwable` except at boundaries
- Use try-with-resources for anything `AutoCloseable` — no manual `finally` close
- Return `Optional<T>` for "maybe absent"; never return `null` from such methods (and never `Optional` for fields/params)

## Idiomatic Patterns

- Use `record` for immutable data carriers — auto `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`, compact canonical constructors for validation
- Use sealed classes/interfaces (`sealed ... permits`) to model closed hierarchies the compiler can reason about
- Combine sealed types + records with pattern matching `switch` for exhaustive, expression-style dispatch
- Use pattern matching for `instanceof` (`if (o instanceof User u)`) and record deconstruction patterns
- Use `var` for local variables when the initializer makes the type obvious — not for fields or public APIs
- Use text blocks (`"""..."""`) for multi-line strings (SQL, JSON, templates)
- Favor the Streams API for transformations; keep pipelines side-effect-free and avoid `forEach` for mutation
- Prefer immutable collections (`List.of`, `Map.of`, `Stream.toList()`) and defensive copies

## Tooling

- Java 21+ LTS; declare the toolchain/`release` in the build to pin the language level
- Build with Maven or Gradle (Gradle with the Kotlin DSL for new projects); commit the wrapper (`mvnw`/`gradlew`)
- Lock dependency versions; use a BOM for coherent multi-module versions
- Format/lint with Spotless (google-java-format / Palantir) + Error Prone or Checkstyle in CI
- Manage JDK versions with SDKMAN! or toolchains rather than the system JDK

## Testing

- JUnit 5 (Jupiter) as the framework: `@Test`, `@ParameterizedTest`, `@Nested`, `@DisplayName`
- AssertJ for fluent, readable assertions (`assertThat(x).isEqualTo(...)`) over bare `assertEquals`
- Mockito for mocks/stubs; prefer constructor injection so collaborators are easy to substitute
- Testcontainers for integration tests against real databases/brokers
- Keep tests in `src/test/java` mirroring the package structure

## Anti-Patterns

- Returning `null` where `Optional` or an empty collection fits
- Mutable shared state without synchronization; pooling virtual threads
- Anemic getter/setter classes where a `record` would do
- Long `if/else instanceof` chains instead of pattern-matching `switch`
- Catch-and-ignore (empty catch blocks); checked-exception laundering into `RuntimeException` without context
